  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 PM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: A mostly quiet afternoon continues in the Tri-State \narea.  A corridor of 40+ knot flow at 850 mb continues to lead to \nweak elevated convection just south of the CWA. While a low risk,\na storm could cross over and enter the CWA into late afternoon.\nTemperatures will continue to rise through the 80s elsewhere, \nlikely topping near 90 degrees along and south of I-90. Dew \npoints remain elevated either side of 70 degrees leading to heat\nindex values in the upper 90s. \n\nTONIGHT:  Models remain a bit split on development later tonight, \npoorly resolving any subtle vorticity in the mid-lvl flow moving \nnortheast after dark.  Some models show a bifurcated LLJ, with one \nbranch centered over NW Iowa and southern MN along a more sustained \nlow-lvl moisture gradient.  A second branch may split into central \nand northern SD after midnight.  Convergence on both these features, \ncombined with very weak dPVA could lead to high based showers and \nthunderstorms after midnight.  Severe weather risks continue to \nremain on the lower side, but MUCAPE lifted off the 850 mb layer \ncould reach as high as 1700 J/KG, with 30-35 knot effective \nshear potentially suggesting some low end rotation potential. A \nfew stronger wind gust may fall out of this high based activity \nas suggested by high res CAM data. \n\nTHURSDAY: Still some uncertainty in Thursday's forecast as mid-lvl \nheights begin to rise further over the Plains.  Any lingering \nvorticity is likely to track northeast of the CWA by mid-day, with a \nsurge in low-lvl warm advection pushing 700 mb temperatures as high \nas 13C over the Tri-state area late in the afternoon.  Guidance \nremains all over the place regarding a stronger shortwave crossing \nthe apex of the ridge mid-day Thursday.  Some CAMs suggest a more \norganized MCS tracking out of eastern ND into central or northern \nMinnesota in the afternoon bringing a risk for convection forming in \nthe evening along or just east of the CWA.  Other CAMs focus on the \nweaker inhibition on the edge of the CAP, forcing a few \nthunderstorms along a weak frontal boundary.  If no afternoon or \nevening convection were to develop, then there remains an outside \nchance for overnight convection along the Ridge/Minnesota river \nvalley after midnight on the LLJ. \n\nFRIDAY-SATURDAY: The mid-lvl ridge axis will remain overhead as we \nfinish the work week end start the weekend.  The result will be a \ncontinuation of very hot and humid conditions for early September. \nThe combination of 850mb temperatures >10 degrees above normal, \nalong with weak surface flow will push high temperatures into \nthe 90s, with pooling dew points in the upper 60s to lower 70s. \nThe resultant heat index values may top the 95 to 105 range, \nwhich could necessitate an advisory for areas along and south \nof I-90. Those with outdoor activities or sporting events should\nuse extra caution given the heat. \n\nLatest trends keep the risk for convection low heading into the\nweekend, but not non-zero. A subtle weakness in the flow moving\nNE through Nebraska late Friday does bring the potential for \nhigh based thunderstorms late in the evening and early \novernight. While instability and shear will be less favorable, a\nfew gusty storms could be possible. Will maintain a low <30% \nPoP at this time. The unknown track of any vorticity topping the\nridge and/or associated MCS activity lowers confidence \nSaturday, but a complex of storms is certainly possible with \nfavored areas just north and east of the CWA on the edge of the \nCAP. \n\nSUNDAY-TUESDAY: No substancial changes to the long term \nportions of the forecast as the ridge begins to slide eastward \nand longwave troughing moves closer to the Rockies. The shift \ntowards southwesterly flow aloft should bring an increased risks\nfor precipitation to the Plains depending on the placement of \nfronts and advection of moisture northward. The increase in \nclouds and moisture should lower temperatures slightly back \ntowards the lower to middle 80s, still slightly above normal.",
